Keep a constraint's constant right-hand side in the bounds and the report.

gnm_solver_update_derived turns constraints on the input cells into the
variables' bounds.  For a constant right-hand side,
gnm_solver_constraint_get_part puts the constant in *cr and no cell in
*rhs; cell_is_constant then overwrote *cr with 0 for the missing cell, so
"A1 <= 3" bounded A1 by 0, and with "Assume Non-Negative" the bounds met
and Gnumeric's own solver refused the problem as an equality constraint.
The program report's gnm_solver_constraint_part_as_str likewise asked
cell_name for the missing cell (a CRITICAL and an empty right-hand side
in the report).  Found through the Cocoa front end on 2026-10-12; the GTK
front end runs the same code.  Leave *pc alone when there is no cell
(get_part set it to 0 for an empty cell and to the constant otherwise),
and write the constant through gnm_solver_constraint_side_as_str.

--- a/src/tools/gnm-solver.c	2026-10-12 18:20:00
+++ b/src/tools/gnm-solver.c	2026-10-12 18:20:00
@@ -485,7 +485,10 @@
 	g_string_append (buf, translate ? _(type) : type);
 	if (gnm_solver_constraint_has_rhs (c)) {
 		g_string_append_c (buf, ' ');
-		g_string_append (buf, cell_name (rhs));
+		if (rhs)
+			g_string_append (buf, cell_name (rhs));
+		else	/* a constant */
+			gnm_solver_constraint_side_as_str (c, sp->sheet, buf, FALSE);
 	}
 
 	return g_string_free (buf, FALSE);
@@ -1470,10 +1473,9 @@
 static gboolean
 cell_is_constant (GnmCell *cell, gnm_float *pc)
 {
-	if (!cell) {
-		*pc = 0;
+	/* No cell: an empty one, or a constant the caller put in *pc.  */
+	if (!cell)
 		return TRUE;
-	}
 
 	if (cell->base.texpr)
 		return FALSE;
